        "descriptions": [
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "These are novel datasets of longitudinal panel survey data (i.e., repeated surveys of the same people) when a hurricane is threatening and approaching landfall in the mainland U.S. and after the hurricane has dissipated. Three survey waves were collected pre-landfall when the hurricane was threatening (the threat / predictive phase), and one survey wave was collected post-storm (post landfall or dissipation). The three waves of data collected during the predictive phase measured what forecast and other risk information respondents were getting, respondents' risk perceptions, and respondents' protective behavioral responses, all pertaining to the hurricane risks at that time. The data collected during the post-storm wave correspondingly measured what forecast and other risk information was most useful and what people's actual experiences were with the hurricane. Such rapidly deployed, event-based, repeated-measure data for hurricanes are novel. This project includes datasets for the following hurricane events: Hurricanes Laura and Marco in 2020, Hurricane Henri in 2021, and Hurricane Ian in 2022.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          },
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "The goal of this mission was to create a comprehensive database of all voluntary and mandatory evacuation orders issued in response to Hurricane Ian (2022) in the state of Florida.  The main source of information for this dataset was acquired from the Florida Department of Emergency Management via an information request.  This data was then compared to two published EO existing datasets for Hurricane Ian (HEvOD and a dataset collected by A. Schumacher) to ensure there were no verifiable omissions.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          }
        ],
